{%- comment -%}
  PG Reviews Text: lift the review copy off the dark canvas.

  pg-landing was written for a white page, so the review block carries light-page
  colours: `.pgx-card-txt{color:#2f2f2f}`, `.pgx-rev-count{color:#333}`,
  `.pgx-hist-n{color:#555}`. pg-dark repaints the card surfaces to #16101F but
  never restates those, so near-black text ends up on a near-black card -- which
  is why the reviews are the hardest thing on the page to read.

  Review text goes white, names pure white, and the supporting figures (review
  count, histogram counts) to the same muted lavender the rest of the dark skin
  uses for secondary copy. The quote carousel above the buy box gets the same
  treatment so both review surfaces match.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------- THE LAYOUT

  It also owns how many reviews sit in a row, for the same reason it owns their
  colour: the rule that decides it is one line of a 40KB file that is otherwise
  nothing to do with reviews, and this file is already the reviews' override
  layer, already global, and small enough to redeploy without risking the rest.

  pg-landing sets `.pgx-cards{columns:4 260px}` and then `columns:1` under
  900px, so every review below that width ran the full width of the page and
  the section became one tall column to scroll past. Now it steps: four across
  on a wide screen (unchanged), three on a tablet, two on a phone.

  `columns: <count> <min-width>` takes whichever gives FEWER columns, so the
  count is a ceiling and the min-width is what actually decides at a given
  width. Multi-column is kept rather than swapped for a grid so the cards stay
  masonry -- a two-line review does not stretch itself to match a six-line one
  beside it, which is what a grid row would force.

  Specificity, not source order, is what makes this win: pg-landing's rules are
  a bare class inside a media query, these are id-scoped, so they hold wherever
  in the document this file happens to render.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
/* review cards */
html body #pgx .pgx-card-txt,
html body #pgx .pgx-card-txt *{color:#F1EDF7 !important}
html body #pgx .pgx-card-name{color:#FFFFFF !important}

/* score block and histogram */
html body #pgx .pgx-rev-num{color:#FFFFFF !important}
html body #pgx .pgx-rev-count{color:#D3C9E4 !important}
html body #pgx .pgx-hist-n{color:#C1B4D2 !important}
html body #pgx .pgx-bar{background:rgba(255,255,255,.12) !important}

/* "Write a review" */
html body #pgx .pgx-write a{color:#C79BEA !important;border-color:#8B44BE !important}

/* the quote carousel in the buy column */
html body #pgx .pgx-quote-txt{color:#F1EDF7 !important}
html body #pgx .pgx-quote-name{color:#FFFFFF !important}
html body #pgx .pgx-quote-next{color:#C79BEA !important}

/* the rating line under the title */
html body #pgx .pgx-rating,
html body #pgx .pgx-rating strong,
html body #pgx .pgx-rating span:not(.pgx-stars){color:#F1EDF7 !important}

/* ---------------- how many reviews sit in a row ---------------- */

@media (max-width: 900px){
  html body #pgx .pgx-cards{columns:3 200px;column-gap:14px}
}

@media (max-width: 600px){
  /* two to a row on a phone. The card lands around 165px wide there, so the
     type steps down with it -- at pg-landing's 15px the words break every three
     or four to a line and each card reads as a column of fragments. */
  html body #pgx .pgx-cards{columns:2 140px;column-gap:10px}
  html body #pgx .pgx-card{padding:12px;margin-bottom:10px;border-radius:12px}
  html body #pgx .pgx-card-name{font-size:14px;margin-bottom:4px}
  html body #pgx .pgx-card .pgx-stars{font-size:12.5px}
  html body #pgx .pgx-card-txt{font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;margin-top:6px}
  html body #pgx .pgx-card-pics img{width:44px;height:44px}
}
</style>
{% schema %}
{"name": "PG Reviews Text", "settings": []}
{% endschema %}
